  <p>
    Plato is the thinker who kept the question open. Aristotle gave us the
    Golden Mean,the right amount in the middle. Lao Tzu showed that opposites lean
    on each other. Plato asked the oldest question of all:is what you are looking at
    right now the whole story,and is there something underneath that is more real? A child can hold that question,and it is worth holding.

  <h2>The cave</h2>
  <p>
    Plato's best-known image is a cave. Picture people chained in place since
    childhood,facing a wall. Behind them,a fire,and things pass in front of it,
    so all they ever see is shadows moving on the wall. Growing up in there,they
    would take the shadows for the whole world. They would name them,argue about
    them,and bet on which shadow came next.
  <h2>The Forms</h2>
  <p>
    Plato's second big idea is that some things have a perfect version that no one
    ever sees. A circle you draw is never a perfect circle,no matter how steady
    your hand. There is no perfect circle in the world,and yet you can imagine
    one,and you can tell a not-quite circle from a close one. Plato called that
    perfect,unseen version the Form of the thing,the circle that is really a
    circle,in the same way you can know what is fair without ever meeting fairness
    with a face.
  <h2>Why asking beats being told</h2>
  <p>
    Plato practiced this as a method,and it is the piece you can use tonight.
    Instead of handing a child an answer,you hand them a question,and you wait.

    "What do you think happens after?","Why do you think that?"That gap between
    the question and the answer,when the child has to supply the missing part,is
    where thinking starts. Plato believed you cannot pour understanding in
    someone the way you pour water in a cup. You have to draw it out of them
    with questions. That is why he wrote dialogues instead of lectures:people
    talking,disagreeing,and changing their minds out loud,and you watch them do it,and
    learn to do it too. It is the oldest argument in the world that a question is
    worth more than an answer,and it has held up a very long time.K

  <h2>How to bring Plato to a young child</h2>
  <p>Three ways that do not mention the word Plato,and still teach the habit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Spot the shadow.</strong> A moving tree's shadow,things looking
      different in low light,a reflection in a window. Point one out and ask:"What is
      making that?"The child learns that what you see depends on where you stand,and there
      is something behind the picture doing the work.K
    <li>
      <strong>Name the invisible version.</strong> When they say a drawing is not
      quite a fair circle,agree,and name it:"There is a perfect circle in your head,and
      you can see yours is not quite it. That is Plato's idea,and you found it
      yourself."
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refuse one answer.</strong> Next time they ask a why question you
      actually know the answer to,give them half of it,and then ask,"What do you
      think the rest is?"Before you supply the rest. You are training them that their
      own guess is worth waiting for.K


  <h2>How each thinker hands a child a tool</h2>
  <p>If you read our Curious Kid books,in one place the Curious Kid meets one
  thinker,and each thinker gives him one tool. This is how the three we have met
  so far split the work:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Thinker</th>
        <th>The tool</th>
        <th>The question it answers</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Aristotle</td>
        <td>The right amount sits between too much and too little</td>
        <td>How much of a thing is right?</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Lao Tzu</td>
        <td>Opposites lean on each other</td>
        <td>What does this need its opposite for?</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Plato</td>
        <td>What you see is not the whole story</td>
        <td>What is underneath what I am looking at?</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Each one is one idea,a child can hold. Together they build the same habit:a
    child who does not settle for the first answer.K

